<% content_for :title, 'Woods Map · refused' %>
<div class="empty-state refusal-panel">
  <div class="eyebrow">HTTP 403</div>
  <h1 class="t2">Woods Map refused the request</h1>
  <% case @reason %>
  <% when :host %>
    The host's <code>config.authorize</code> hook did not allow this request. Sign in to the host application with an account the hook accepts, then open the map again.
  <% when :origin %>
    The request came from another origin. Writes to the map are accepted from its own origin only; open the map directly and try again.
  <% when :rebinding %>
    The request arrived over loopback but named a host that is not loopback, which is how a DNS-rebinding page would reach the map through this browser. Open the map at <code>localhost</code> or <code>127.0.0.1</code>, list the name in <code>config.allowed_origins</code>, or configure <code>WOODS_MAP_TOKEN</code>.
  <% else %>
    This host is not loopback and no <code>WOODS_MAP_TOKEN</code> is configured, so the map does not boot its routes. Set the token and open <code>/woods?token=…</code> once; it becomes a cookie. <code>config.authorize</code> can put the host's own sign-in in front of the token; it does not replace it.
    <pre class="cmd" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Token and hook configuration">WOODS_MAP_TOKEN=$(openssl rand -hex 24)   # staging only
# config/initializers/woods_map.rb
WoodsMap.configure do |c|
  c.authorize = ->(r) { r.env["warden"].user&.admin? }
end</pre>
  <% end %>
  <div class="cap">No index data, no unit names and no paths appear on this page; it is served before the map opens anything.</div>
</div>
